{
    "title": "Scope of Federal Regulations Implementing the Surface Mining Control and Reclamation Act of 1977",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "November 24, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-11-24/pdf/2025-20835.pdf",
    "summary": "Two federal moves landed today: an emergency safety order from the aviation agency that grounds certain airplanes until they pass inspections, and a pair of housekeeping rules from the Interior Department that delete outdated mining regulations.\n\nThe aviation action is immediate and strict. Starting December 1, specific aircraft cannot fly until they\u2019re inspected and any fixes are made. Limited exceptions may be allowed, and alternative compliance methods can be approved by the agency\u2019s safety office, but the default is \u201cno flight until safe.\u201d Who benefits: passengers, crews, and the broader public, because it lowers risk right away. It also gives airlines clear marching orders and a single point of contact for approvals. Who might be negatively affected: airlines and operators facing sudden schedule disruptions, added maintenance costs, and potential cancellations; travelers who may see delays or fewer flights; and smaller carriers that have less flexibility to swap aircraft. Maintenance providers could see short\u2011term demand spikes, which helps them but may strain capacity.\n\nThe mining rules are about cleanup, not new policy. Interior is removing references to a research grants program that left the agency decades ago and is deleting procedures for distributing replacement funds that were fully paid out years back. This is presented as a noncontroversial update to keep the rulebook current. Who benefits: state and tribal programs, companies, and lawyers who want fewer obsolete citations to trip over; federal staff who spend less time maintaining dead text; and anyone who values a clearer regulatory map. Who could be negatively affected: essentially no one in practice, though people or organizations that still cite the old provisions will need to update references. Any unspent grant dollars from the past will continue to follow the rules that applied when they were awarded.\n\nUnless significant objections arrive by December 24, these Interior changes take effect January 23, 2026. Comments can be filed through the government\u2019s rulemaking portal.",
